    <h1>Convert JSON records to CSV without damaging IDs</h1>
    <p><strong>Reviewed 30 October 2026.</strong> JSON API exports often contain optional properties and nested data. CSV is easier to inspect in spreadsheet applications, but conversion can change how identifiers and special values are interpreted. This guide shows what FilePebble actually exports and what to verify after importing.</p>
    <section><h2>1. Prepare an array of records</h2><p>The JSON to CSV tool expects a non-empty array of objects. Try this input: [{"id":"001","name":"Asha","city":"Mangaluru"},{"id":"002","name":"Ravi","city":"Udupi","active":true}]. Keeping id as a string preserves the leading zeros at the JSON stage.</p></section>
    <section><h2>2. Understand the columns</h2><p>The converter collects fields from the records, including optional properties. This example produces columns id, name, city and active. The first row has a blank active cell; the second row contains true. A missing value is not proof that the source had a literal empty string—it may mean the property was absent.</p></section>
    <section><h2>3. Handle nested values</h2><p>A property such as {"preferences":{"theme":"dark"}} becomes JSON text inside one CSV cell; it does not automatically create preferences.theme and other separate columns. If you need flattened columns, transform the JSON structure before converting.</p></section>
    <section><h2>4. Open identifiers as text</h2><p>Excel and other spreadsheets may infer the CSV column type and turn 001 into 1. Import the CSV with id as a text column rather than double-clicking it and relying on automatic detection. If the original JSON stores a very large ID as an unquoted number, JavaScript numeric precision can already affect it before conversion; use strings for exact identifiers.</p></section>
    <section><h2>5. Understand spreadsheet formula safety</h2><p>Cells that look like spreadsheet formulas may be prefixed to reduce formula-injection risks when opened in spreadsheet software. That is helpful for casual viewing but can change the exact exported cell text. Review the CSV before using it in an automated import that requires byte-for-byte source values.</p></section>
    <section><h2>6. Validate the result</h2><p>Check headers, missing fields, quoted commas and several representative rows in a text editor before opening a spreadsheet. For the reverse workflow, use CSV to JSON, which keeps CSV values as strings. This tool does not convert full Excel .xlsx workbooks or preserve formulas or formatting.</p></section>
    <h2>Try the workflow</h2>
    <p><a href="/tools/json-to-csv/">Open the related FilePebble tool</a>. Keep an original copy of important files, inspect the downloaded result, and report unexpected behavior through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
